"Mark M." <anonymous@westshore.com> writes:
> Can I 'tar' the files to a single tar file rather than tape. Then
> copy the single tar file to the other machine. Then un-tar it?
> Presently I tar it to tape, then drive the tape to the other
> machine, hook it up, then read it from tape.

Sure--just do something like

tar cf archive.tar subdir

That tar's everything in the directory 'subdir' into a file called
archive.tar (make sure you don't write the tar file to the directory
that you are archiving--or it may try to archive itself!).  rcp or ftp
that to the remote system, and untar it with

tar xf archive.tar

You can even eliminate the intermediate file (this is a more advanced
Unix idiom--you might want to experiment a bit to get comfortable with
the concepts before trying this in a production environment):

tar cf - subdir | rsh remotehost '(cd /path/on/remote/host ; tar xf -)'

That tar's up subdir and writes it to stdout, which is piped into an
rsh to remotehost.  The shell on remotehost cd's to /path/on/remote/host,
and unpacks stdin (your tar "file"!) into /path/on/remote/host/subdir.

If you have a slow link and fast cpu's, you can compress it along the
way

tar cf - subdir | compress | \
     rsh remotehost '(cd /path/on/remote/host ; zcat | tar xf -)'

Pipes let you do all sorts of tricky things--learning a few of the
common idioms can save you a lot of work.
-- 
