I have never taken measurements, but I expect the performance to 
increase with growing block size on all kinds of tape devices 
and all Unix variants 
until you reach some block size which is "optimum" (or close to). 
Until now, all drives I used showed such a behaviour. 

One reason might be the overhead per block - just remember it is 
a system call to read from the tape with all its cost in CPU, 
context switch, telling the controller, ... 
With small blocks, you will not get the tape drive to "stream", 
and start-stop is a severe performance loss. 

However, 256,000 byte is very large, and I would not go to such 
extremes for fear of meeting a device or OS which will not 
handle it. Compaq/Digital claim Tru64 to safely handle 64 kB 
= 65,536 byte (maybe more), but the 'tapex' test program has a 
limit at 64,xxx byte (= slightly below 64 kB). Hence I have set my 
'dump' block size to 48 kB (large enough for streaming, but small 
enough to allow checking by 'tapex -m -w'). 

Regards, Joerg Bruehe 


