Adam Howell <ahowell@vehicle.me.berkeley.edu> writes:
> Feb 23 07:53:04 vehicle vmunix: Defering I/O (errno 5) for block(0x130,
> 0x130) on device 8,34818

For some reason, the OS couldn't write to a disk, so it deferred the
IO operation.  To find out which disk, do

ls -l /dev | grep '8, *34818'

That should identify a device name, which you can match up with a
partition via df or 'cat /etc/fstab'.  Next thing to do is check the
error logs, via "/usr/sbin/uerf -R -o full | more" and see if there
are error log entries for that device.  If, for example, device
8,34818 is rz18c, then you should look in the error log for errors on
SCSI bus 2 device 2 (bus*8 + device).  Where you go from there depends
on the type of error.  If you are getting request timeouts, the disk
may be hanging or otherwise going offline (we have some Micropolis
3391s that do this).  Short term, you might be able to avoid the
reboot by power cycling the disk and/or using scu to reset the device,
but I don't guarantee the integrity of the data on the disk if you do
this.  Long term, you may be able to fix the problem by shuffling
which controller the device is on, or you may have to replace the disk
drive.
-- 
