> a.  Should I make 2 copies of the software on the 2 nodes ??? or should
> I put only 1 on the virtual IP ??This software was never tested on a
> cluster anyway..

It depends on many things.
Do you wan't to make a standby cluster (1 service on one machine) or do
you want to run both nodes at once with one or more services ?

If you want the Informix DB available at all times, you need to make a
disk service and place the DB on the cluster disk. This way, the DB (the
files) will allways be available to the machine running the actual
service.

A simple explanation of a ASE disk service is that it's some disk space
and a IP alias that can be located on one member in a cluster at a given
time.
If you need the disk to be available on both machines at the same time,
you need to do some ugly NFS thingy, or look at unix 5.0.
 
> b. Is there any extensive material on the web reguarding cluster
> anagement ????Specifically concerning the problem above ??

Hmmm, I know there's alot of internal Compaq stuff, but I don't remember
any public links.
 
> c.  What is the effect of the software running on 2 nodes simultaneously
> ? Is this feasible ???

if it uses alot of CPU power it's great, but if it just asks the DB to
do something and the DB is the only CPU hog, then it's not... then you
need to split up your DB and put something on one machine and the rest
on the other in two or more services.

Th fist solution is easy to implement. The other is a bit more tricky if
you only have one big database that can't be easily split up in logical
parts.
I've recently been involved a project where we made 4 ASE disk services
on two machines (4 because we're then able to expand to 4 machines
later). On the two machines we run about 30 Oracle databases, so we just
took 1/4 of them and put on each service, made some customisation to
make failover work smooth and we then have full use of the 4 CPUs in
each machine, and if one machine crashes, the two services will failover
to the other machine, and it'll then run all 4 services alone.


l8r/Jspr



