NNTP-Posting-Date: 25 Feb 2000 16:18:29 GMT
Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1,comp.windows.x

Wondering if anyone has seen this before.

I have an application that writes rotated text (90, 180, or 270
degrees) using the following steps:

1) Create a pixmap (XCreatePixmap) to hold the text
2) Write the text (XDrawImageString) to the pixmap
3) Get the pixels from the pixmap (XGetImage)
4) Rotate the image data
5) Write the rotated pixels to the window (XPutImage)

This has worked without problems on all kinds of X-servers.  We are
now experiencing random core dumps with Xdec under OSF1 V4.0D.  From
an analysis of the core dump, it appears that Xdec is writing over
areas of memory with data intended for the pixmap.  Further testing
shows the following pattern:

String            Result (in pixmap after XDrawImageString)
A                 A
AB                AB
ABC               ABC
ABCD              ABCD
ABCDE             ABCDE
ABCDEF            ABCDEF
ABCDEFG           ABCDEFG
ABCDEFGH              EFGH
ABCDEFGHI            DEFGHI
ABCDEFGHIJ          CDEFGHIJ
ABCDEFGHIJK        BCDEFGHIJK
ABCDEFGHIJKL          EFGHIJKL
ABCDEFGHIJKLM        DEFGHIJKLM     (the server crashes around here)
ABCDEFGHIJKLMN      CDEFGHIJKLMN
ABCDEFGHIJKLMNO    BCDEFGHIJKLMNO

Once the string is longer than 7 characters, the first (4 - (N % 4))
characters are apparently not drawn to the pixmap, but instead into
some random part of the server memory.

The workaround I am using is to make "N" calls to XDrawImageString
each with length one, instead of one call with length "N" (i.e. draw
the text into the pixmap one character at a time).  This seems to
work, but we only figured it out yesterday and haven't done extensive
testing.

Has anyone seen this before?  Known problem?  Patches available?

-- 
Dan



