Mark M. <anonymous@direct.ca> wrote:
>I know a few things about Unix and have worked with it as a low-level
>technical user for a number of years, but we have someone who is good at
>LINUX who could maybe help lock things down in the OSF/1.

First, make sure you have (at least) all the security-related patches
installed.  This is true for most any OS.  You can get the patches
from ftp://ftp.service.digital.com/public/unix/.  Patches are
in subdirectories by OS version.

Turn off all services you don't need running in /etc/inetd.conf, or
stop the service if it's run from the /sbin/rc?.d/ scripts.

Consider using ssh (or something like it) that encrypts traffic in
case someone does get in, and runs a sniffer on your network.  If
you do this turn off the telnet/login/shell services in /etc/inetd.conf.

Security is not a one-time thing - you have to keep checking your
systems, applying patches as necessary, etc.


