In article <38B3B548.C8E70D51@iona.com> Brian Buckley <brian.buckley@iona.com> writes:
>I am trying to get a c function call for what should be really simple -
>getting the start time of a process (any process, i.e. of which I am not
>the owner).
>
>This is no problem in SVR4 systems with a procfs (solaris, IRIX, Linux)
>- just stat() the corresponding /proc entry for the process.  Even AIX
>which has no procfs at least has a getprocs() system call.  My system
>here has a procfs but for some reason which baffles me it updates the
>creation time of the /proc entries every minute (does anyone know
>why???) - so stat() is useless.  I have searched the documentation for
>something like AIX's getprocs() to no avail.

Brian,

The procfs file system in Tru64 Unix sets all three fields (mtime, atime,
and ctime) to the current time. This is the behaviour you're seeing.

You can use table(TBL_UAREA) to get the U area for a process, which
contains a field u_start.

