Long long ago in a galaxy far far away...

OSF created OSF/1, a set of operating system pieces and parts vendors
could use to make their own commercial operating system. Somewhere, I
think the idea was to provide a common (not standard) basis for
UNIX-like operating systems (contrast to POSIX). From the DUNIX FAQ
(http://ftp.digital.com/pub/Digital/dec-faq/Digital-UNIX):

	It is important to understand what OSF/1 is and has always 
	been. It is not a finished operating system but a set of
	operating system technology components, from which vendors 
	can pick and choose pieces of technology that complement 
	their product development strategies. Many UNIX system 
	vendors use elements of OSF/1 code -- Hewlett-Packard in 
	HP-UX, IBM in AIX, and many others, including Digital in 
	Digital UNIX. 

	Digital uses components of OSF/1 technology, just as 
	it integrates technology from other suppliers with its 
	own internal development. By following this strategy of
	integrating needed components, Digital has been able to 
	produce the best implementation of the UNIX operating system 
	in the industry and to bring it to market quickly.

If you continue to read the paragraph, you find that it says the initial
release of DUNIX was not dependent on OSF/1 code. In otherwords, OSF/1
was probably used as an example, but by the time it was released it was
a 100% (well, 99%) original piece of work.

More information can probably also be found at the OSF web site
(http://www.osg.org ... Which isn't responding for me... Has it
changed?).

