 >> <usman@thynne.psych.nwux.edu> wrote:
 >>>Tru64 4.0 - I can't see a /dev/stderr in the device
 >>>hierarchy. On other unices (linux, solaris) it's usually
 >>>a symlink to the device that corresponds to fd 2.
 >>>Can I create it myself?
 >>>
 >>>Isn't this POSIXish? Say I have a Bourne shell script
 >>>whose output I want to direct to stderr or a file or
 >>>/dev/null, depending on a switch. How can I do it if
 >>>there's no way to reference /dev/stderr ?
 >> 
 >> In the Bourne or Korn shells if you want to redirect I/O to stderr,
 >> you do the following:
 >> 
 >>     command args 1>&2
 >> 
 >This was my first idea, too, but it won't help if you want to do
 >something like
 >
 >  command args 2> $logfile
 >
 >where $logfile is either /dev/null or stderr: > and >& are different
>redirection operators, and unfortunately, they can't be the result
 >of a variable expansion. /dev/stderr is there for a reason.
 >
 >Some (clumsy) workarounds:
 >  - use
 >      lsof -p <PID> -a -d 2
 >    to determine what descriptor 2 is actually connected to,
 >  - or use a pipeline like
 >      {
 >        command1
 >        command2
 >        ...
 >      } 2>&1 | {
 >        case $logfile in
 >          stderr )
 >            cat >& 2
 >          ;;
 >          * )
 >            cat > $logfile
 >          ;;
 >        esac
 >      }
                                                              
I didn't say /dev/stdout wasn't a good idea, only that it was not a
standard, which means it is not portable.

If you use the 'eval' command you can get what you want.  I also know
that this is not a straight forward, nor as obvious to the person
reading the script as using a /dev/stderr, but it will work.  For
example:

#-----------------------------------------------------------------------
#!/bin/ksh
STDERR="2>file.stderr"
STDOUT=">file.stdout"
write_stderr()
{
        echo "this is something written to stderr" 1>&2
}
write_stdout()
{
        echo "this is something written to stdout"
}
eval write_stderr $STDERR
eval write_stdout $STDOUT
exit
#-----------------------------------------------------------------------

The above script will be portable, and it will allow the redirection of
stderr and stdout to be placed in a variable that may be changed by the
script depending on conditions.

Using 'eval' also works for the example you give:

        eval command args 2> $logfile

Again, I'm not against /dev/stderr, it just doesn't exist in Tru64 UNIX
and I'm attempting to offer things that will work and things that
should be portable to other operating systems that support the bourne
and korn shells.



