I work once in a while on two DEC Alphastation 166's with OSF/1, (version is
about 2 yrs old). Each system is identical (like two engines on a plane, to
keep it flying in all conditions) because we run a mission-critical Process
Control system on it that operates heavy equipment at a shipping site.

Here's the problem: Every hour of every day a 250k file is created
containing history for the hour (24 files per day). We have about 50,000
files now and it continues to grow on each machine at 24 files per day in a
single directory. When I use "ftp -i" or even "rcp"  to copy files from one
machine to the other it only permits me to copy about 250 to 300 files at a
time, (I use a wildcard when transferring). If my wildcard is going to
access more than this number of files I get some sort of error saying that
"it's too much to do at once".

I must copy history periodically between machines because sometimes one
machine goes down, and therefore gets a "history hole" that has to be filled
with files from the other machine. Another reason for copying large numbers
of files is when I want to copy a directory within a single machine, and in
this case, the same problem occurs.

Is there a setting I can change on the system so that I can copy as many
files as I want? This seems like a ridiculous limitation we are living with.
To compensate for this limitation I have been saving massive directories
with TAR, then restoring the directories all at once to the second machine,
(but this is also slow since the machines are about a kilometre apart,
connected by fibre).

Mark M.





