This is a "Kludge" but can you use 'rsh' to either start a script
running on the remote system or to pass commands over to the remove
system to be run.

If you can, then may be you could use 'find' to collect the names of
the files to transfer and then have the remote system push them over to
you via 'rcp' or an 'ftp' utility running in a script.

Or use 'rsh' to run 'find' on the remote system and return the list of
files to the local system where you can run 'rcp' using the list of
files returned.

this is just psuedo shell code which I have not debugged nor I have I
run it.  It is just to give an idea of what I mean.

#!/bin/ksh
rsh -l username remote_host find /directory_of_histories -print | \
while read file
do
    rcp remote_host:$file local_destination
done
exit

In this case the 'find' command is shipped to the remote system, but
its output is passed back to the local system where it is piped into a
shell script 'while' command that reads each filename into the variable
'file'.  The 'while' loop then executes an 'rcp' command for each file
to be copied.

You could just as easily pass over all the commands to be executed on
the remote, or you could copy over a script to find the files and have
the remote system copy them over to your local system.

You could also pass over the 'ls -1' command to get a list of files in
the directory.

Your 'while' loop can do some filtering based on the names if that is
desired.  The 'find' command can do various selection services such as
only selecting files that less than a day old 

        find /dir -mtime -3 -print

I hope this helps.



