In article <87p8nk$e42$1@neptunium.btinternet.com>,
Brett Shepherd <bretts@btinternet.com> wrote:
> Hi All,
> 
> I am fairly new to Digital Unix, being from an HP background.
> 
> One of our 8400's is having an STK9710 tape library (6 x DLT4000 drives)
> attached to it over this weekend.
> 
> My query is this:
> 
> Once attached, how do I identify the new tape drives (and presumably the
> robotic arm) to the system? Is it really a case of identifying the Bus/SCSI
> ID and then running a MAKEDEV for each? Do I need to do anything else? Is
> there any way of getting DU to do the work for you? On HP it would simply be
> case of running 'ioscan' (to pick up the devices) and then 'insf' (to
> install the special files - based on the ioscan).

Brett-

You don't say what version of Digital Unix you're using, but if it's 4.x or
later, the tape devices should show up "trivially".  Doing a reboot
with the tape drives on the bus(es) will probably cause them to show up.

The changer is another story.  Like HP-UX, you need "changer" support in
the kernel.  You'll want to load the CLCMC3XX fileset, which is the 
"SCSI CAM Medium Changer Driver".  That adds kernel support for the media
changer (robot) and also adds a few entries to /etc/ddr.dbase.   Part of
installing the CLCMC subset will be to build a new kernel.  Once you've
rebooted with that I'm pretty sure you will need to make the media changer
device yourself, by hand, using the /dev/MAKEDEV.mc script.  The invocation
is

	cd /dev
	./MAKEDEV.mc mcUNITNUM

where UNITNUM is

	(8 * bus #) + SCSI ID

i.e. if your changer is on bus #3 at id 6, it would be

	(8 * 3) + 6 = 30

so you would

	./MAKEDEV.mc mc30

Tim

