From: "Adam Price" <adam@pappnase.demon.co.uk>
Subject: 3rd RFD: comp.unix.tru64 (renames comp.unix.osf.osf1, remove comp.unix.osf.misc)
Date: 2000/02/09
Message-ID: <950056469.11699@isc.org>
X-Deja-AN: 583453489
Archive-Name: comp.unix.tru64
Approved: newgroups-request@isc.org
Followup-To: news.groups
Newsgroups: news.announce.newgroups,news.groups,comp.unix.osf.osf1,comp.unix.osf.misc,comp.sys.dec,comp.unix.misc

                     REQUEST FOR DISCUSSION (RFD)
    unmoderated group comp.unix.tru64 (renames comp.unix.osf.osf1)
        REMOVE comp.unix.osf.misc (renamed to comp.unix.misc)

This is a formal Request For Discussion (RFD) for the creation of a
world-wide unmoderated Usenet newsgroup comp.unix.tru64 and the
removal of the Usenet newsgroup comp.unix.osf.misc.  This is not a
Call for Votes (CFV); you cannot vote at this time.  Procedural
details are below.

CHANGES from previous RFD:

At the request of several interested parties the scope of the RFD was
extended to include the removal of comp.unix.osf.misc and as such the
rationale for the removal of comp.unix.osf.misc is new.

Following comments received in the first discussion phase the
exclusion for binaries in the charter was re-written.

The following line was added to the charter for comp.unix.tru64:
Complaints about the off-charter nature of other posts are, whilst
understandable, also unwelcome.

A comment about traffic levels has been added to the rationale for
the rename of comp.unix.osf.osf1 at the request of Meg Worley.

A second proponent (Dan Riley) was added.

Newsgroup line:
comp.unix.tru64		Discussion of Tru64 Unix.

RATIONALE: comp.unix.tru64

Compaq Tru64 UNIX is among the oldest 64bit unix, yet there is no
obvious and specific place to discuss it, or ask questions regarding
it's use and administration.

The current places where Compaq Tru64 UNIX is discussed are not
intuitively named for this purpose, and as a result, new users and
admins are confused as to where to post and either ask in general
newsgroups, or not at all.

Traffic in comp.unix.osf.osf1 is healthy, and there is no reason to
believe that the name change would reduce this.

RATIONALE: REMOVE comp.unix.osf.misc (renamed to comp.unix.misc)

During the RFD for renaming comp.unix.osf.osf1 concern was raised
that the newsgroup comp.unix.osf.misc would be left orphaned by
the renaming. It was pointed out by several people that this group
did not seem to receive any on-topic traffic, and that what traffic
there was seemed to be cross-posted to other groups.

So the question is whether there will be enough legitimate non-tru64
traffic to justify retaining any comp.unix.osf group.

To attempt to answer this, the the last 30 days of both comp.unix.osf
groups were examined. No on-topic non-tru64 posts were found. It would
be surprising if there were more than 2 or 3 non-tru64 osf posts in
the last year, and a search of Deja.com's archive failed to find a
single post within the charter of the group (although of the 600 or so
posts archived, only the first 300 or so were reviewed before a
problem with deja.com caused the search to be abandoned). There is a
perception that traffic has been declining and it seems unlikely to
increase, since the OSF has become 'The Open Group' and discontinued
all operating system development.

Jim Riley wrote a short summary of traffic in message
<873m7b$l8b$1@nntp8.atl.mindspring.net> From which he concluded...
>Almost everything appears to be either cross-posted to cu.osf.osf1 or
>have a link to Digital, Alpha's, or Tru64.  The exceptions are the two
>off-topic posts, and the post about looking for a Solaris administrator
>(which might have been posted to comp.unix.*).  There were a couple of
>people who went out of their way to specify that they were running
>Digital UNIX and which Alpha models, but this may have been just for
>completeness, with the critical element which version they were
>running.

He also stated in the same message
>By its charter, comp.unix.osf.misc, is not appropriate for OSF/1
>discussion, but was intended for follow on products.  It was created
>as part of a reorganisation that create several newsgroups that were
>very finely distinguished (e.g. comp.unix.sys5.v3 and
>comp.unix.sys5.v4 and comp.unix.sys5.misc).  It is quite possible if
>there had ever been an OSF/2 that it would have been discussed in
>cuo.osf1 rather than cuo.misc as the authors of the original split had
>anticipated.

Here are all the possible OSF Unix implementations that could be found
by the proponents:

SCO - mentioned in response to the RFD.  As far as we can tell, while
SCO has used some OSF technology, they never produced an OSF/1 based
product.

IBM - IBM may have used OSF/1 as the base for a version of AIX (the
360 series mainframe version?).  It is suspected that any posts go to
the AIX groups, and certainly could do so after the removal.

hppa1.1-hp-osf, hppa1.0-hp-osf - Found in the gcc config.guess,
possibly one of the OSF reference ports.  The proponents do not recall
seeing any questions about the hppa port in comp.unix.osf.*.

DECstation - Digital originally intended to produce a DECstation
version of OSF/1 to replace Ultrix.  This project was cancelled before
any official releases, but questions about the beta releases have been
infrequently posted to c.u.o.osf1.  The rate of such posts has
declined to near vanishing in recent years.

i?86-unknown-osf1 - Found in the gcc config.guess. No traffic was
seen, however the proponents suggest that any traffic could be
directed to comp.unix.misc or to a group devoted to the type of
problem such as comp.unix.admin.

i860-intel-osf1 - the Intel Paragon massively parallel supercomputer,
based on OSF1/AD.  Very few deployed, all at places like Los Alamos,
never seen a post in c.u.osf.*.  Would arguably be the only OS that
would be on topic in cuo.misc, but supercomputer users generally have
other avenues of support.

mklinux - Linux on the OSF1/AD Mach 3.0 micro-kernel, ported to Apple
PPC and HPPA-RISC.  Appears to be Linux to the user.  Proponents
suspect all the posts end up in the Linux groups since none are
remembered or archived in cuom.

Other OSF technologies - the other widely deployed OSF technologies
are Motif, DCE and CDE, all of which have their own newsgroups:
comp.windows.x.motif, comp.soft-sys.dce, comp.unix.cde.

Historically, the proponents do not believe that significant non-Tru64
OSF1 traffic has been present in recent years, and do not see where
additional traffic might come from.  We expect that if an osf group
were left, it would be dominated by Tru64 questions just as the
current groups are.  If there were evidence, either historical or from
people's expressed interest, that there would be non-Tru64 traffic on
a c.u.osf group the proponents would suggest one be created during the
reorganisation; in the absence of any such evidence it is argued that
the hierarchy be entirely removed.

CHARTER: comp.unix.tru64

The purpose of the group is to provide a place for discussion of
issues relating to owning, operating, managing, administering and
using computer systems running versions of the operating system now
officially called Compaq Tru64 UNIX, previously called DIGITAL UNIX,
previously called DEC OSF/1.

Binary, HTML, and other types of non-plaintext material are
prohibited with the exception of PGP signatures or similar small
binaries. Posters are requested to keep these binaries as small
as possible.

Job postings are unwelcome.

Adverts if posted at all should be short and of the nature of a
pointer to information about a product rather than complete details,
should be in direct response to a query, and should not be repeated
more than once per month.

Complaints about the off-charter nature of other posts are, whilst
understandable, also unwelcome.

END CHARTER.

PROCEDURE:

This is a request for discussion, not a call for votes.  In this phase
of the process, any potential problems with the proposed newsgroups
should be raised and resolved.  The discussion period will continue
for a minimum of 21 days (starting from when the first RFD for this
proposal is posted to news.announce.newgroups), after which a Call For
Votes (CFV) may be posted by a neutral vote taker if the discussion
warrants it.  Please do not attempt to vote until this happens.

All discussion of this proposal should be posted to news.groups.

This RFD attempts to comply fully with the Usenet newsgroup creation
guidelines outlined in "How to Create a New Usenet Newsgroup" and "How
to Format and Submit a New Group Proposal".  Please refer to these
documents (available in news.announce.newgroups) if you have any
questions about the process.

DISTRIBUTION:

In addition to the five groups in the newsgroups line (comp.sys.dec,
comp.unix.osf.misc,comp.unix.osf.osf1,news.announce.newgroups and
news.groups) this RFD has been reposted in comp.unix.admin,
comp.unix.ultrix, comp.unix.admin, comp.unix.misc, vmsnet.misc and
comp.os.vms at the request of respondents to the first RFD.
For all of the above follow-ups are directed to news.groups.

Proponent: Adam Price <adam+usenet@pappnase.demon.co.uk>
Proponent: Daniel S. Riley <dsr@mail.lns.cornell.edu>



