Technically it is already somewhat different.
You need a cluster password to join the VMS cluster,
but you need almost nothing to mount a NFS disk.
You need a lot of expertise to write software to access a VMS
cluster disk without being a cluster member,
but everybody can buy a Linux CD-rom, install Linux on its
hard disk and mount the NFS disk.

But more important are non-technical issues.
For some reason the Linux community is different.
Owners of VMS systems accept central control of their system.
Linux users want to control their systems themselves,
but at the same time they want to use central facilities.
We want to see how others solve this.
NIS and NFS are popular. Do Linux users at other sites accept it
that they don't have root access?

                            F.Z.




