In article <38999c74.4130269@news.earthlink.net>, mtanquary@earthlink.net (Matt Tanquary) writes:

>I removed a drive which recently failed and replaced it with another.
>The old drive was a Seagate Barracuda 4.5 gig. The new drive is an IBM
>DNES-309170 9.1 gig. The SCSI ID is 5, on bus 'scsi1'.
>
>I rebuilt the device drivers for rz5&rrz5, but disklabel still gives
>me the following:
>
>>># disklabel -rw rz5 not_in_drivetab             
>>>disklabel: /dev/rrz5a: No such device or address

According to the intro(2) manual page, the error you got can be
cause by the following conditions.

[ENXIO]   No such device or address
      Indicates one or more of the following errors:

        +
        The specified address, major device number, or channel is out of
        valid range.

        +
        No more channels are available (open()).

        +
        The named file is a character-special or block-special file and the
        associated device does not exist (open()).

        +
        The O_NONBLOCK flag is set, the named file is FIFO, O_WRONLY is set,
        and no process has the file open for reading (open()).

        +
        The specified tape drive is offline or a disk pack is not loaded in
        the specified disk drive.

The most likely case, is that the kernel doesn't recognize the drive
or it isn't ready.  This being a SCSI drive you can use the scu(8)
command to pound on it in some detail; do an Inquiry command to see
if it responds at all, read a couple of mode pages, send it a Test
Unit Ready (always a good test) and Read Capacity.  This should
offer a clue, since you'll get back the Sense Key, ASC and ASCQ
of any SCSI failures.


>I am also interested in knowing how you can distinguish a drive on
>bus0, id5 from a drive on bus1, id5.

The drive number is found from (bus * 8) + target-id

Generally, it is a good idea, to double check the presense of
drives with the console, since that offers a slightly different
SCSI implementation.

