Our main systems used to be OpenVMS systems in an OpenVMS cluster.
In this way we had a highly secure file server system, where the=20
home directories of all users were located on a few central=20
servers, with a secure authentication system.
Powers beyond our control force us to move from OpenVMS to
Windows and Unix/Linux.
We succeeded to set up a Windows NT domain and we think that
in this way we have a reasonably secure file server system and=20
authentication system for the Windows users.

Now we want to set up a file server system and a centrally managed
authentication system for the Unix (mostly Linux, a few Compaq=20
Tru64) systems at our site. We see that almost everyone uses NFS=20
and NIS for this purpose.=20
We also read in the Security HOWTO section 8.9:

 NIS is not secure at all. It was never meant to be.

In the NFS HOWTO, section 6.2, we find:

 "But the root user on the client can still use 'su' to
 become any other user and access and change that users=20
 files!" say you. To which the answer is: Yes, and that's=20
 the way it is, and has to be with Unix and NFS.

So, even if we find a secure authentication system, even then we
should export NFS systems with user files only to systems completely =
under our control. Owners of PC's should be given=20
access to a remote NFS file system only if they are not able to=20
login as root on their own PC, nor to boot from anything else but=20
the partitions created by us. This will lower the enthousiasm of=20
these users for the file server by a large amount, since one of=20
the charms of using Linux is the possibility for everybody to=20
install his own flavor of distribution, X environment, and other=20
software on his PC.

We know that there are a few alternatives for NIS and NFS, but
we have the feeling that we would place ourselves out of the=20
main stream by using them, which could bring us to a dead end.

Given these security problems and the popularity of NIS and NFS,
we wonder how other peoply look at this. Do they completely trust
their users? Do they remove completely the possibility of their=20
users to manage their own system? Do they silently accept the=20
risks? Do we overlook a possibility?=20

      F.Z.




