In article <389EDF65.C71E1B0@viewlocity.com>, Marcus Johansson
<mjohansson@viewlocity.com> wrote:

>Hi!
>
>I have a Compaq Unix 5.0 server who is constantly complaining about lack
>of swapspace. There is absolutely nothing running on it, and i've
>configured it pretty much default, no strange things at all.
>
>I know this isn't much information, but I don't know what to include..
>Except for this:
>
># bash-2.03# swapon -s
>    Reserved space:         12677 pages ( 77%)
>
>What is reserved space?

Since you have a V5.0 system, you want to use
/usr/bin/X11/dxkerneltuner utility and change the 'vm' parameter
'vm_swap_eager' to zero.  The vm_swap_eager parameter is kept in the
/etc/sysconfigtab file.  On V4.0 and earlier versions, specifying eager
vs lazy was done by having a symbolic link named /sbin/swapdefault
which pointed to your swap device if you wanted eager swapping.  In
V5.0 this has been replaced with the sysconfigtab 'vm_swap_eager'
parameter.

The next time you reboot your system, processes will only allocate
space in swap file when they are actually swapped.

Currently in eager mode, the applications reserver as much space in the
swap area as they have virtual memory, even if they are never swapped.

If you have lots of memory and do not swap very often, then turning off
eager swap mode can allow you to get away with having much less swap
space.

However, if you do end up needing to swap processes, then if you do not
have sufficent swap space for all the virtual memory of all the
processes that need to be swapped, then the system will be forced to
actually kill processes that need to swap but can not get the swap
space to be swapped into.  That is the trade-off you have to make when
deciding on using eager vs lazy swap modes.

Personally on my workstation, I use lazy swap mode, but I also have a
reasonable amount of swap space and I keep some spare disk partitions
hanging around just in case I need to add some swap space.  It happens
from time to time, like when I decided to play with the 'xv' utility on
a huge picture image, or start too many compiles and links at the same
time, or Netscape has yet another memory leak and eats up too much
memory and increases swapping on other processes, etc...

But for the most part, lazy swap mode (vm_swap_eager=0) is not a
problem for me.  Your mileage may vary.



