It's actually pretty similar on dunix.
Below is a mail I posted a couple of weeks ago around here (or was it
com.sys.dec...). It's actually an explanation in adding disks while
being in production, but it's the same procedure when it comes to tape
drives, just replace rz with tz :


Do a "scu scan edt" to scan for new stuff.
Do a "scu show edt" to see what's on the SCSI bus.

Your disk should be in the list you get.
To play safe you can let scu scan only the bus the disk is on rather
than all. Normally it's not an issue.


cd to /dev

do a ./MAKEDEV rzXY


You calculate XY like this :

SCSI bus 0, ID 0 -> 7 = rz0 -> rz7
SCSI bus 1, ID 0 -> 7 = rz8 -> rz15
SCSI bus 2, ID 0 -> 7 = rz16 -> rz23

...and so on, assuming you aren't playing with LUN numbers, or the disk
is on a RAID controller.
If it's a cluster and the disk is on a shared bus, the SCSI numbers will
most likely be renumbered. This is no problem seen from the OS, but if
you try to do a real life calculation of the number of SCSI controllers
and ID's to get the rz name, you'll get confused.

LUN numbers will show up as letters after the rz but before the number.
LUN 0 (no LUN) doesn't have a letter (ex. rz129) LUN 1 is b (rzb129),
LUN 2 is c (rzc129) and so on.
Example from /var/adm/messages (it's a ES40 ASE cluster with
HSG80/ESA12000 storage) :

Dec  5 15:14:29 subra vmunix: Alpha boot: available memory from
0x65a8000 to 0x13fff0000
Dec  5 15:14:29 subra vmunix: Digital UNIX V4.0F  (Rev. 1229); Wed Nov 
