Do a file /dev/rrzXY to se you got the device file created correctly.

Then do a disklabel -r rzXY just to see you got the right disk. It
shouldn't have a disklabel if it's new.
Just to be sure do a disklabel -z on it, especially if it's a used disk
and it has a disklabel. The disklabel might be wrong and origin from a
stripeset or something. It'll seem to work but it really doesn't.

Then do a disklabel -wr -t advfs rzXY AdvFS (or whatever you normally
use for default disklabel).

Then change the disklabel with disklabel -e rzXY if you need to.

The rest of the procedure (ie. putting AdvFS, LSM or UFS on it) is
probably wellknown to you.


l8r/Jspr



