> What is essential difference between
>         - Compaq Tru64 Unix
>         - Solaris/SunOS
>         - HP UX
>         - SCO Unix?


Trying to start a "religious" war ???!!!

I manage 50+ Tru64 systems and 20+ Solaris systems.  The "alphas" range
from the old DEC 3000 series (note I did not say DECstation) to the 
new ES40.  The "sparcs" are all Ultra 1, Ultra 30 and Ultra 60.

From a hardware point, the alphas kick ass !  On a CPU per CPU basis 
nothing Sun (or HP or IBM) can touch them (check www.spec.org).

From an OS standpoint OSF1/Digital Unix/Tru64 has obvious BSD root
(which I personally like) while Solaris is System 5.

I personally believe that Tru64 is easier to administer. All
"subsystems" 
(ie NFS, NTP, NIS, SMTP, etc.) have setup scripts that are very simple 
(assuming you know the answers !!).  Tru64 is POSIX/xpg4 compliant "out 
of the box".

Solaris is the "classic" "voodoo, high priest" style of administration.
Not nearly as easy to install/configure subsystems.  Some things are
hidden (why isn't there a /etc/init.d/nis ?).  POSIX/xpg4 is clearly
an "afterthought".

For companies that buy telephone support contracts I rate DEC (yes, I
still call them DEC) a 11/10 and Sun 1/10 (I work for a large company
that literally buys/leases 100's of new Suns every year and almost 0
Compaq equipment, and we still get better service from them).

The unfortunate truth is, much more 3rd party software runs on Solaris
than Tru64.  Seeing as all computers are just "applications" you must
buy the one that "makes your toast" regardless of price/perfomance
or support.



Jack Patteeuw



