From: John Kohl <jtk@rational.com>
Subject: C compiler optimization of inline memcpy()
Date: 2000/02/22
Message-ID: <6cbt5957h8.fsf@rational.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 588531365
X-Us-Snail: Rational Software Corp., 20 Maguire Road, Lexington, MA  02421-3104
Sender: jtk@fructose
Organization: Rational Software Corporation
Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1

Is this a legitimate optmization, or a compiler gone too far?

This compiler:
	% cc -V
	cc  (cc)
	        Tru64 UNIX Compiler Driver 5.0
	Compaq C V6.1-013 on Digital UNIX V5.0 (Rev. 910)

with these flags:
	% cc -migrate -c -fast -tune ev5 -arch ev4 -g3 foo.c

generates different code for inline memcpy() depending on the type of
the source pointer in the call.  If the type is (unsigned long *), then
it assumes the pointer value is aligned and can be dereferenced as-is.
If it's (char *), it uses unaligned loads and bit shifts to effect the
copy. (See the annotated assembly output below.)

The manual declares memcpy() as:
  void *memcpy(
          void *s1,
          const void *s2,
          size_t n);
and says memcpy() conforms to ISO C, XPG4, and XPG4-UNIX.

Is it legitimate for the compiler to generate ldq instructions to
dereference the pointer?

Witness:

#include <string.h>

extern void
bar(unsigned long **x);

unsigned long
foo_long(char *abc)
{
    unsigned long *foo;
    unsigned long retval;

    bar(&foo);
    memcpy(&retval, foo, sizeof(unsigned long));
    return retval;
}

unsigned long
foo_char(char *abc)
{
    unsigned long *foo;
    unsigned long retval;

    bar(&foo);
    memcpy(&retval, (char *)foo, sizeof(unsigned long));
    return retval;
}

