> 
> and "df" lists the filesystems doing what you'd expect:
>         Filesystem              [...]           Mounted on
>         root_domain#root        [...]           /
>         usr_domain#usr          [...]           /usr

You might have a h partition too. A disklabel -r rz8 will show if the g
partition eats all the rest of the disk or not.
A normal recent Compaq default install will be 256 MB on /, 1, 2 or 3
times the memory on b depending on how much memory you have, 2 GB on g
and what's left on the disk on h.
 
> But clicking on rrz9 in diskconfig, gives you:
> 
>         This disk has not been labeled.  Select and apply one of the
>         default partitions.
> 
> I'd like to mirror the setup on rrz8, so the two domains and swap get
> covered by software RAID 1.  Is this possible without a reinstall?

Yes, you need LSM (Logical Storage Manager).
LSM is allready available on your machine, but to do mirroring you need
a LSM-OA license. LSM without a license will only encapsulate, not
mirror, stripe, concat or anything else.
Do a "lmf list" to see what licenses you have.

The exact procedure is :

Setup the rz9 disk with a disklabel identical to rz8
Encapsulate the rz8 disk using volencap
Mirror rz8 to rz9 using volrootmir

After this procedure rz8 & rz9 will be in sync.
Be advised that volencap of the root disk _can_ fail if something is
setup wrong. If it fails, you need to know your dunix pretty well to get
back to a working system and get volencap right the next time.


The you need to tell the hardware it's able to boot from one of two
devices.
In the boot-console you'll have a parameter named bootdef_dev. It's
probably set to dka100 (rz8). You need to change that so it have both
rz8 & rz9 :

set bootdef_dev dka100,dka200 (I don't remember if the, should be a
space instead...)

Also auto_action should be set to boot or restart.



l8r/Jspr



